{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788205100315,"sessionID":"ses_fa6ae4622ffeRIXSVkVPYYmujp","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_6e63ac4c1f214cc4b5562395","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n# Unique tournaments in BR-Football\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    tourns = {}\n    n = 0\n    for row in r:\n        tourns[row['tournament']] = tourns.get(row['tournament'], 0) + 1\n        n += 1\nprint(\"BR-Football rows:\", n)\nfor t, c in sorted(tourns.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1]): print(f\"  {t!r}: {c}\")\nEOF"},"output":"BR-Football rows: 10296\n  'Serie A': 3689\n  'Serie B': 3677\n  'Serie C': 1807\n  'Copa do Brasil': 1123\n","metadata":{"output":"BR-Football rows: 10296\n  'Serie A': 3689\n  'Serie B': 3677\n  'Serie C': 1807\n  'Copa do Brasil': 1123\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv\n# Unique tournaments in BR-Football\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    tourns = {}\n    n = 0\n    for row in r:\n        tourns[row['tournament']] = tourns.get(row['tournament'], 0) + 1\n        n += 1\nprint(\"BR-Football rows:\", n)\nfor t, c in sorted(tourns.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1]): print(f\"  {t!r}: {c}\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788205100313,"end":1788205100315}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The MCP API works: `MCPServer`, `@app.tool()`, `app.run()` (stdio default). Dicts get JSON-serialized as TextContent.\n\nNow let me design the full implementation:\n\n**Architecture** (flat, in current directory):\n\n```\nbrazilian_soccer_mcp/\n  __init__.py\n  config.py         — paths, constants\n  normalize.py      — team name normalization + date parsing + text utils\n  loader.py         — load all 6 CSVs into unified Match records + Player records\n  knowledge_graph.py— in-memory knowledge graph (nodes/edges) built from data\n  queries.py        — query layer: matches, teams, players, competitions, stats\n  server.py         — MCP server with tools\n  __main__.py       — python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp\ntests/\n  conftest.py\n  test_normalize.py\n  test_loader.py\n  test_queries_matches.py   (BDD GWT style)\n  test_queries_teams.py\n  test_queries_players.py\n  test_competitions.py\n  test_stats.py\n  test_server.py    (end-to-end MCP tool calls)\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\npytest.ini or pyproject\n```\n\n**Unified Match model:**\n- competition (brasileirao | copa_do_brasil | libertadores | extended/other), date (ISO), home_team (normalized canonical name), away_team, home_goals, away_goals, season, round/stage, source file, extra stats (corners, shots, attacks for BR-Football), arena (novo campeonato), state info.\n\n**Team name normalization:** This is the crux. Examples:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"Palmeiras\"\n- \"Sport-PE\" → \"Sport\"\n- \"América - MG\" → \"América-MG\" / \"América Mineiro\"\n- \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → \"Boavista\"\n- Full names like \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → \"Corinthians\"\n- Accented: \"São Paulo\", \"Grêmio\", \"Avaí\", \"Vitória\", \"Ceará\", \"Fortaleza\", \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atlético-PR\" vs \"Athletico Paranaense\" vs \"Atlético Mineiro\" vs \"Atlético Goianiense\"...\n- \"Vasco\" vs \"Vasco da Gama\"\n- Known alias map for major clubs.\n\nStrategy:\n1. Strip state suffix: `-SP`, `- RJ`, ` (antigo ...)`, etc. (split on '-' only when trailing token is a 2-letter state uppercase, careful: \"Athletico-PR\" → \"Athletico\"; \"Grêmio\" has no dash; \"América - MG\" → \"América\").\n2. Remove accents, lowercase, remove punctuation for the canonical key.\n3. Alias mapping for common variants: \"vasco\" → \"Vasco da Gama\"? Hmm — careful. Better to keep a canonical display name table for the big clubs and fall back to the cleaned name.\n\nAlias normalization approach:\n- Clean: remove accents → ascii, lowercase, strip state suffixes, strip parentheticals, remove \"fc\", \"ec\", \"esporte clube\", \"sport club\", \"sc\", etc.? Careful: \"Sport-PE\" → \"Sport\" is the actual club (Sport Recife). If I strip \"sport club\" from \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" I get \"corinthians paulista\" → map \"corinthians\". \n\nI'll build an alias dict for the top clubs mapping normalized keys → canonical display names:\n- \"palmeiras\" → \"Palmeiras\"\n- \"flamengo\" → \"Flamengo\"\n- \"fluminense\" → \"Fluminense\"\n- \"vasco\" / \"vasco da gama\" / \"vasco da gam\" → \"Vasco da Gama\"\n- \"corinthians\" / \"corinthians paulista\" / \"sport club corinthians paulista\" → \"Corinthians\"\n- \"sao paulo\" / \"são paulo\" → \"São Paulo\"\n- \"santos\" → \"Santos\"\n- \"gremio\" → \"Grêmio\"\n- \"internacional\" → \"Internacional\"\n- \"atletico mineiro\" / \"atletico-mg\" / \"atletico mg\" / \"atlético mineiro\" → \"Atlético Mineiro\"\n- \"athletico paranaense\" / \"athletico-pr\" / \"athletico pr\" / \"atletico paranaense\" / \"ca paranaense\" → \"Athletico Paranaense\"\n- \"atletico goianiense\" / \"atletico-go\" → \"Atlético Goianiense\"\n- \"bahia\" → \"Bahia\"\n- \"sport\" / \"sport recife\" → \"Sport Recife\"\n- \"cruzeiro\" → \"Cruzeiro\"\n- \"fortaleza\" / \"fortaleza esporte clube\" → \"Fortaleza\"\n- \"ceara\" → \"Ceará\"\n- \"goias\" → \"Goiás\"\n- \"botafogo\" → \"Botafogo\"\n- \"bragantino\" / \"red bull bragantino\" → \"Bragantino\"\n- \"figueirense\", \"chapecoense\", \"avai\" → \"Avaí\", \"vitoria\" → \"Vitória\", \"juventude\", \"atletico paranaense\"...\n\nAlso foreign clubs in Libertadores: \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\" → strip country suffix \"(URU)\" or \"-EQU\". Handle \"Club Atlético ...\" etc. For non-Brazilian, just clean suffixes and keep.\n\nSince the graph should preserve the \"canonical\" name, I'll:\n- `strip_suffix(name)`: remove trailing \"-XX\" where XX is state/country code (2-3 uppercase letters), remove parenthetical content.\n- `normalize_key(name)`: unaccent, lowercase, strip non-alnum → single space.\n- `canonical(name)`: check alias map first (on normalized key), else return cleaned display name (title-ish preserving accents from original cleaned string).\n\nFor the display name, better keep original cleaned text (with accents) when no alias matches.\n\n**Date parsing:** formats:\n- \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\"\n- \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\"\n- \"29/03/2003\" (DD/MM/YYYY)\n- \"2023-09-24\"\n- Brasileirao CSV also might have weird stuff. Let me scan the data for distinct date formats to be safe.\n\n**Unified competitions:**\n- Brasileirão_Matches.csv → \"Brasileirão Série A\"\n- Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv → \"Copa do Brasil\"\n- Libertadores_Matches.csv → \"Copa Libertadores\"\n- BR-Football-Dataset.csv → tournament column (Copa do Brasil, Brasileirão Série A, Copa Libertadores, Copa do Nordeste, etc.) — extended stats.\n- novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv → \"Brasileirão Série A\" (2003-2019) — overlapping with Brasileirao_Matches.csv (2012+)! There may be duplicates. I should dedupe? The task says all 6 files loadable and queryable. For standings calculations, duplicates would skew. I'll load both into separate \"datasets\" but the unified match list can include a `source` field and dedupe key (competition+date+home+away). I'll implement dedupe: if same normalized teams, same date, same competition → keep first (prefer the one with more info). Actually to be safe for stats correctness, dedupe matches on (competition, season, round, home, away) or (date, home, away, competition).\n\nLet me check the data overlap: novo_campeonato covers 2003-2019; Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012+. So overlap 2012-2019. I'll dedupe.\n\n**Knowledge graph:** Build an in-memory graph:\n- Nodes: Team, Player, Competition, Season(?), Match. Keep it simple: teams, players, competitions, matches as nodes; edges: `played_in` (team→competition), `participated_in_match` (team→match), `member_of_club` (player→club), `beat`/`drew_with` (team→team per match with match ref)... To keep memory sane (10k matches × edges fine — ~25k edges).\n- Provide `kg_search` tool: search nodes by name substring; `kg_relations` maybe.\n\nThe MCP server tools (each answering the query categories):\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20)` → list of matches + head-to-head summary when two teams.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` → record.\n3. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None)` → W/D/L, GF/GA, home/away splits, win rate.\n4. `team_season_record(team, season)` — can be folded into team_stats.\n5. `standings(competition, season)` → computed table (Brasileirão).\n6. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, limit=20)` → FIFA data.\n7. `player_profile(name)` → full attributes.\n8. `top_players(nationality/club, order_by=overall, limit)`.\n9. `competition_stats(competition, season=None)` → avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins, total matches.\n10. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)`.\n11. `compare_teams(team_a, team_b, season=None)` → side-by-side + H2H.\n12. `kg_search(query, limit)` → graph nodes (teams/players/competitions) by name.\n13. `kg_neighbors(node)` → relations for a node (teams played, players at club, rivalries...).\n\nPerformance: load CSVs once at server startup; index matches by team-pair, by team, by season. 18k player rows fine. Load time should be a couple of seconds with csv module (fifa file is 9MB, ~18k rows, ~90 cols — fine).\n\n**BDD tests:** pytest-bdd is not installed. Options: install pytest-bdd (network works). TASK says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" and gives gherkin. Installing pytest-bdd and writing .feature files + steps would satisfy this nicely. But pytest-bdd version compatibility with pytest 9.1.1 — pytest-bdd 8.x supports pytest 8; need to check pytest 9 compat. Risky. Alternative: write a tiny GWT harness in tests (`given/when/then` style plain pytest) with feature/scenario docstrings. Safer: plain pytest classes named per scenario with Given/When/Then step methods. Hmm, but \"BDD GWT structured PyTest\" (from prompts.txt, which is ignorable) — TASK.md itself says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios:\" with gherkin examples. It doesn't mandate pytest-bdd specifically.\n\nLet me try installing pytest-bdd and check compat quickly; if it fails, fall back to a hand-rolled GWT style. Actually, simplest robust approach: hand-rolled GWT structure — a `bdd.py` helper with `Scenario` class providing given/when/then methods, and test functions written as:\n\n```python\ndef test_find_matches_between_two_teams(load_data):\n    \"\"\"Feature: Match Queries — Scenario: Find matches between two teams\"\"\"\n    given = Given(match_data_loaded())\n    when = when_(given.search_matches_between(\"Flamengo\", \"Fluminense\"))\n    then = then_(when.matches_returned())\n```\n\nThat gets contrived. pytest-bdd is the real deal; let me try to install it. If network allows (it did for mcp), fine.\n\nLet me try: pip install pytest-bdd. Check compat with pytest 9.1.1.\n\nThen tests:\n\n```gherkin\nFeature: Match Queries\n  Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n    Then I should receive a list of matches\n    And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n```\n\nwith step defs.\n\nLet me also verify data quirks: check goal columns for Libertadores (strings \"2\"), missing values, weird dates. Let me scan the CSVs a bit more.\n\nAlso BR-Football-Dataset: `tournament` values include \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Brasileirão Série A\"? Let me check unique tournaments. And team naming there (\"Sao Paulo\" without accent). Also dates in it: \"2023-09-24\". And novo_campeonato: dates \"29/03/2003\", plus OBS column. Vencedor: Mandante/Visitante/Empate.\n\nLet me scan unique values quickly to design normalization properly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_059546cd0001Ek034RxDlCD8rI","sessionID":"ses_fa6ae4622ffeRIXSVkVPYYmujp","messageID":"msg_0595352db001U79Hzg0rLyVs7e"}}
